// The Modern World, part 1: global challenges and how societies are changing — migration, hunger, water, the energy transition, the Sustainable Development Goals, inequality, population, megacities, ageing and fast fashion.
ADDART({id:"migration",t:"Migration and Refugees",c:"Economics and Society",i:"🧳",w:"Human migration",r:["ofw","un","hrights","climchange","inequality"],
p:[["People on the move","Hundreds of millions of people live outside the country where they were born."],["Why people move","People migrate for work, study, family and safety."],["Refugees","Refugees are people forced to flee war or persecution."]],
l:"Migration is the movement of people from one place to another to live, either within their own country or across borders. Today more than 300 million people, about one in every 30, live outside their country of birth. Most move for work, study or family, but tens of millions have been forced from their homes by war, persecution, violence or disasters, including refugees who have crossed borders to seek safety. Migrants send money home, fill jobs and enrich cultures, while migration also raises debates about borders, integration and rights. The Philippines is one of the world’s great migrant-sending countries.",
s:[["Why people migrate","People are pushed to leave by poverty, unemployment, conflict, persecution or disasters, and pulled toward places with jobs, safety, education or family. Most migrants move to nearby countries, and many more move within their own countries, especially from the countryside to cities."],["Refugees and asylum","A refugee is a person who has fled their country because of a well-founded fear of persecution or because of war. The 1951 Refugee Convention protects refugees from being sent back to danger. People who apply for protection are called asylum seekers, and the UN Refugee Agency, UNHCR, helps refugees worldwide."],["Forced displacement","More than 100 million people worldwide have been forced from their homes. Most are internally displaced people, who remain inside their own countries. Large displacement crises in recent years have affected people from Syria, Afghanistan, Ukraine, Venezuela, Sudan, Myanmar and elsewhere."],["Remittances","Migrants send money home to their families, called remittances, totaling hundreds of billions of US dollars a year to low- and middle-income countries, more than foreign aid. India, Mexico, China and the Philippines are among the largest recipients."]],
k:[["International migrants","More than 300 million"],["Refugee Convention","1951"],["UN Refugee Agency","UNHCR"],["Forcibly displaced people","More than 100 million"],["International Migrants Day","18 December"]],
d:[["1950","The UN Refugee Agency is created"],["1951","The Refugee Convention is adopted"],["1974","The Philippines begins its overseas employment program"],["1990","A UN convention on the rights of migrant workers is adopted"],["2015","More than a million migrants and refugees reach Europe by sea"],["2018","The Global Compact for Migration is adopted"]],
s2:[["Filipinos around the world","Millions of Filipinos live and work abroad as nurses, caregivers, seafarers, engineers, domestic workers and in many other jobs, in the Middle East, Asia, North America and Europe. Their remittances support families and make up a significant part of the Philippine economy. Their sacrifices and the long separations from families are part of modern Filipino life."],["Who hosts refugees","Contrary to common belief, most refugees live in low- and middle-income countries near their homelands. Countries such as Iran, Türkiye, Colombia, Germany, Uganda and Pakistan have hosted some of the largest refugee populations."],["Climate and migration","Rising seas, droughts, floods and storms are increasingly forcing people to move. Most climate-related displacement happens within countries. Low-lying Pacific nations such as Tuvalu and Kiribati are planning for a future in which some citizens may need to relocate."],["Debates about migration","Supporters say migrants fill labor shortages, pay taxes, start businesses and bring new ideas and cultures. Critics worry about pressure on wages, housing and public services, illegal border crossings and social cohesion. Governments try to balance border control, economic needs and humanitarian duties, and views differ widely."],["Dangerous journeys","Migrants without legal routes often make dangerous journeys across deserts, jungles and seas, sometimes at the mercy of smugglers and traffickers. Thousands die each year crossing the Mediterranean Sea and other routes."],["Brain drain and brain gain","When skilled workers such as doctors and nurses leave, their home countries can lose talent, called brain drain. But returning migrants and diaspora communities can bring back skills, investment and connections, sometimes called brain gain."]],
sp:["The Global Compact for Migration","In December 2018, more than 150 countries met in Marrakesh, Morocco, and adopted the Global Compact for Safe, Orderly and Regular Migration, the first international agreement covering all aspects of migration. It is not legally binding but sets out shared goals, such as saving lives on migration routes, fighting human trafficking, reducing the costs of sending remittances and helping migrants integrate. Some countries refused to join, saying it threatened their sovereignty, while supporters saw it as a step toward cooperation on an issue no country can manage alone."],
g:[["Migrant","A person who moves to live in another place"],["Refugee","A person forced to flee their country because of persecution or war"],["Asylum","Protection given by a country to a refugee"],["Remittance","Money sent home by a migrant worker"]],
f:["More than 300 million people live outside their country of birth.","The Refugee Convention was adopted in 1951.","The Philippines is one of the largest receivers of remittances."],
fun:"Remittances to developing countries are larger than all foreign aid combined.",
tl:"Ang migrasyon ay paglipat ng tao sa ibang lugar o bansa upang manirahan. Mahigit 300 milyong tao ang nakatira sa labas ng bansang sinilangan nila. Ang mga refugee ay napilitang tumakas dahil sa digmaan o pag-uusig. Milyun-milyong Pilipino ang nagtatrabaho sa ibang bansa."});

ADDART({id:"foodsecurity",t:"Hunger and Food Security",c:"Economics and Society",i:"🥣",w:"Food security",r:["agri","rice","greenrev","climchange","inequality","foodwaste"],
p:[["Enough food for all","Food security means everyone always has enough safe, nutritious food."],["Hunger today","Hundreds of millions of people still go hungry."],["Causes","Conflict, climate shocks and poverty are the main drivers of hunger."]],
l:"Food security exists when all people, at all times, can get enough safe and nutritious food for an active and healthy life. Although the world produces enough food to feed everyone, about 670 million people, around one in twelve, faced hunger in 2024, while many more could not afford a healthy diet. Hunger is driven mainly by conflict, climate shocks and economic crises, and it harms children’s growth and learning most. Ending hunger is the second of the United Nations’ Sustainable Development Goals.",
s:[["Hunger and malnutrition","Hunger is not having enough food. Malnutrition includes undernutrition, such as stunting, when children are too short for their age, and wasting, when they are too thin, as well as deficiencies in vitamins and minerals. At the same time, overweight and obesity are rising in many countries, a double burden."],["Why people go hungry","Most hungry people live in places affected by war, where farms, markets and roads are destroyed. Droughts, floods and storms ruin harvests. Rising food prices and poverty mean families cannot afford enough food even when it is available."],["Famine","Famine is the most extreme form of hunger, when many people die from starvation and disease. Experts use a scale called the Integrated Food Security Phase Classification to measure food crises. In recent years famine conditions have been confirmed in parts of Sudan and Gaza, caused by conflict and blocked aid."],["Food waste","Roughly a fifth to a third of food is lost or wasted, from farms to homes, while people go hungry. Reducing waste, improving storage and helping smallholder farmers could feed many more people."]],
k:[["People facing hunger","About 670 million (2024)"],["UN goal","Zero Hunger (SDG 2)"],["Main causes","Conflict, climate shocks, economic crises"],["World Food Day","16 October"],["Nobel Peace Prize","World Food Programme (2020)"]],
d:[["1945","The Food and Agriculture Organization is founded"],["1961","The World Food Programme is created"],["1966","IR8, a high-yielding rice from Los Baños, helps launch the Green Revolution in Asia"],["1996","The World Food Summit defines food security"],["2015","Zero Hunger becomes a global goal"],["2020","The World Food Programme wins the Nobel Peace Prize"]],
s2:[["Children","Children suffer most from hunger. Undernutrition in the first 1,000 days, from pregnancy to age two, can cause lasting damage to growth and brain development. Stunting affects about one in five children under five worldwide, and school feeding programs help keep children healthy and in school."],["The Philippines","Many Filipino families face food insecurity, especially during typhoons and price spikes. Stunting has long affected a large share of young children. The Philippines is one of the world’s largest rice importers, so world rice prices strongly affect Filipino households. Feeding programs, rice subsidies and support for farmers aim to improve food security."],["Climate change","Rising temperatures, changing rainfall and more extreme weather threaten crops, livestock and fisheries. Scientists are breeding crops that tolerate drought, flooding and salt, such as flood-tolerant rice developed at the International Rice Research Institute in Los Baños."],["Feeding the future","The world’s population is still growing. Ideas for producing more food sustainably include improving farm productivity, reducing waste, restoring soils, eating more plant-based foods and developing new foods such as cultivated meat, though each approach has supporters and critics."],["Food as a weapon","Blocking food supplies to civilians has been used as a weapon of war throughout history. International humanitarian law forbids starving civilians as a method of warfare, and in 2018 the UN Security Council condemned it."],["Food banks and community kitchens","Communities fight hunger through food banks, soup kitchens and sharing. In the Philippines in 2021, community pantries spread across the country, where people left food for neighbors in need, following the idea of giving according to one’s ability and taking according to one’s need."]],
sp:["The community pantry","In April 2021, during the COVID-19 lockdowns, a young woman named Ana Patricia Non set up a small bamboo cart of vegetables, rice and canned goods on Maginhawa Street in Quezon City, with a sign inviting people to give what they could and take only what they needed. Photos spread online, and within days hundreds of community pantries had appeared across the Philippines, run by neighbors, farmers, churches and students. The movement showed how ordinary people could respond to hunger with solidarity, and similar pantries appeared in other countries."],
g:[["Food security","Reliable access to enough safe, nutritious food"],["Malnutrition","Poor nutrition from too little, too much or the wrong food"],["Stunting","Being too short for one’s age because of poor nutrition"],["Famine","Extreme food shortage causing widespread deaths"]],
f:["About 670 million people faced hunger in 2024.","World Food Day is 16 October.","The World Food Programme won the 2020 Nobel Peace Prize."],
fun:"The Philippine community pantry movement began with one bamboo cart in 2021.",
tl:"Ang food security ay ang pagkakaroon ng sapat, ligtas at masustansiyang pagkain ng lahat sa lahat ng oras. Mga 670 milyong tao pa rin ang nagugutom. Ang digmaan, pagbabago ng klima at kahirapan ang pangunahing sanhi."});

ADDART({id:"waterscarcity",t:"The Water Crisis",c:"Geography",i:"🚰",w:"Water scarcity",r:["sanitation","wcycle","climchange","agri","water"],
p:[["Not enough water","Billions of people face water shortages for part of the year."],["Safe to drink","Around two billion people lack safely managed drinking water."],["Using water wisely","Farming uses most of the world’s fresh water."]],
l:"Water scarcity is a lack of enough fresh water to meet people’s needs. Although water covers most of the Earth, only a small fraction is fresh water that people can easily use, and it is unevenly spread. Around two billion people lack safely managed drinking water, and billions experience severe water shortages for at least part of the year. Growing populations, farming, industry, pollution and climate change are putting increasing pressure on rivers, lakes and underground water. Solutions include saving water, fixing leaks, recycling wastewater, protecting watersheds and, in some places, removing salt from seawater.",
s:[["How much fresh water?","About 97 percent of the Earth’s water is salty seawater. Of the remaining fresh water, most is frozen in ice caps and glaciers or hidden deep underground. Only a tiny share is in lakes, rivers and shallow groundwater that people can easily use."],["Where water goes","Farming uses about 70 percent of the fresh water taken from rivers, lakes and aquifers, mostly to irrigate crops. Industry and energy production use about a fifth, and homes about a tenth."],["Kinds of scarcity","Physical scarcity happens in dry places where there is simply not enough water. Economic scarcity happens where water exists but people lack pipes, wells or money to access it. Pollution can make available water unsafe."],["Groundwater","Underground water stored in rock layers called aquifers supplies drinking water for billions of people and much irrigation. In many places it is being pumped faster than rain can refill it, causing wells to dry up and land to sink."]],
k:[["Share of Earth’s water that is fresh","About 3%"],["Fresh water used by farming","About 70%"],["People without safely managed drinking water","Around 2 billion"],["World Water Day","22 March"],["Metro Manila’s main water source","Angat Dam"]],
d:[["1977","The first UN water conference is held"],["1993","The first World Water Day is celebrated"],["2010","The UN recognizes the human right to water and sanitation"],["2018","Cape Town narrowly avoids Day Zero"],["2019","Metro Manila suffers a water shortage"],["2023","The second UN water conference is held"]],
s2:[["Day Zero","In 2018, after three years of drought, the city of Cape Town in South Africa warned of Day Zero, when taps would be turned off and people would collect water at public points. Residents cut their water use by about half, limiting showers and reusing water, and the crisis was avoided when rains returned."],["Water in Metro Manila","Most of Metro Manila’s water comes from Angat Dam in Bulacan. In March 2019, low water levels at La Mesa Dam and rising demand left many households in the east of the metropolis without water for days. The crisis led to plans for new water sources, such as new dams, and debates over how water services should be run."],["Sinking cities","Pumping too much groundwater makes land sink. Parts of Jakarta, Mexico City and coastal areas of the Philippines, such as parts of Bulacan and Pampanga, are sinking, increasing flooding. Indonesia is building a new capital, Nusantara, partly because of Jakarta’s problems."],["Desalination","Desalination removes salt from seawater to make drinking water. It supplies much of the water used in countries such as Israel, Saudi Arabia and the United Arab Emirates. It is reliable but uses a lot of energy and produces salty waste that must be managed."],["Hidden water","Producing food and goods uses large amounts of water, called virtual water. Producing a kilogram of beef can take thousands of liters, mostly to grow animal feed, while a cotton T-shirt can take more than 2,000 liters. Choices about food and clothing affect water use around the world."],["Shared rivers","Many rivers cross borders, such as the Nile, the Mekong and the Indus, and countries sometimes disagree over dams and water sharing. Ethiopia’s Grand Ethiopian Renaissance Dam on the Nile, completed in 2025, caused years of tension with Egypt and Sudan, showing why cooperation on shared water is vital."]],
sp:["Saving water at home","Simple habits can save large amounts of water. Turning off the tap while brushing teeth can save several liters each time. Fixing a dripping faucet can save thousands of liters a year. Taking shorter showers, using a dipper and pail instead of a long shower, collecting rainwater for plants and washing full loads of clothes all help. In many Filipino homes, the tabo and timba, the dipper and pail, have long been an efficient way of bathing that uses far less water than a shower."],
g:[["Water scarcity","Not having enough fresh water to meet needs"],["Aquifer","An underground layer of rock that holds water"],["Desalination","Removing salt from seawater"],["Virtual water","Water used to produce food and goods"]],
f:["Only about 3 percent of Earth’s water is fresh.","Farming uses about 70 percent of fresh water.","World Water Day is 22 March."],
fun:"Cape Town residents halved their water use in 2018 to avoid Day Zero.",
tl:"Ang kakulangan sa tubig ay kawalan ng sapat na tubig-tabang. Halos 2 bilyong tao ang walang ligtas na inuming tubig. Agrikultura ang gumagamit ng karamihan ng tubig-tabang. Sa Metro Manila, nagkaroon ng krisis sa tubig noong 2019."});

ADDART({id:"energytransition",t:"The Energy Transition",c:"Technology",i:"🔋",w:"Energy transition",r:["renew","solarpower","ev","fossilfuels","climchange"],
p:[["From fossil fuels to clean energy","Countries are shifting from coal, oil and gas to cleaner energy sources."],["Solar on the rise","Solar and wind power have become the cheapest electricity in many places."],["A big challenge","The change raises questions about cost, jobs and reliability."]],
l:"The energy transition is the worldwide shift from fossil fuels such as coal, oil and gas toward low-carbon energy sources such as solar, wind, hydropower, geothermal and nuclear power, together with electric vehicles, better batteries and more efficient use of energy. Its main aim is to cut the greenhouse gas emissions that cause climate change, while also reducing air pollution and dependence on imported fuels. Solar and wind power have grown rapidly as their costs have fallen, but fossil fuels still supply most of the world’s energy, and the pace, cost and fairness of the transition are widely debated.",
s:[["Why change?","Burning fossil fuels releases carbon dioxide, the main cause of global warming, and pollutes the air, contributing to millions of deaths each year. Under the 2015 Paris Agreement, countries pledged to limit warming to well below 2 °C and to try for 1.5 °C, which requires cutting emissions sharply."],["Cheaper clean power","The price of solar panels has fallen by around 90 percent since 2010, and wind power has also become much cheaper. In many countries, new solar and wind farms are now the cheapest way to generate electricity. China installs more solar and wind power than any other country."],["Electrifying everything","Much of the transition involves using electricity from clean sources for things that once used fossil fuels: electric cars, buses and motorcycles, heat pumps for heating and cooling, and electric cooking. Sales of electric cars have grown to a large share of new cars in China, Europe and elsewhere."],["Storing energy","Because the sun does not always shine and the wind does not always blow, energy storage is essential. Large batteries, pumped hydropower and stronger power grids help balance supply and demand."]],
k:[["Goal","Cut greenhouse gas emissions"],["Key agreement","Paris Agreement (2015)"],["Fastest-growing source","Solar power"],["Solar panel cost fall since 2010","About 90%"],["COP28 pledge","Triple renewable capacity by 2030"]],
d:[["1954","The first practical silicon solar cell is made"],["1997","The Kyoto Protocol is agreed"],["2015","The Paris Agreement is adopted"],["2020","The Philippines stops approving new coal power plants"],["2023","Countries agree at COP28 to transition away from fossil fuels"],["2025","Renewables overtake coal in global electricity generation in the first half of the year"]],
s2:[["The Philippines","The Philippines relies heavily on imported coal and oil for electricity, but it has long used geothermal and hydropower. In 2020 it stopped approving new coal power plants, and it aims to raise the share of renewable energy in its electricity to 35 percent by 2030 and 50 percent by 2040. Large solar farms, offshore wind projects and rooftop solar are expanding."],["Critical minerals","Batteries, solar panels and wind turbines need minerals such as lithium, cobalt, nickel, copper and rare earth elements. The Democratic Republic of the Congo produces most of the world’s cobalt, and Indonesia and the Philippines are leading nickel producers. Mining these minerals raises concerns about environmental damage, workers’ rights and local communities."],["A just transition","Coal mining and power plants employ many workers, and some communities depend on them. A just transition means helping these workers and regions find new jobs and opportunities, so that the costs of change are shared fairly."],["Debates","Supporters argue the transition will create jobs, clean the air, lower energy costs in the long run and protect the climate. Critics raise concerns about the cost, the reliability of power supplies, the speed of change and the burden on poorer countries and households. Developing countries argue that rich countries, which caused most past emissions, should help pay."],["Nuclear power","Nuclear power produces large amounts of electricity without carbon emissions. Some countries are building new reactors or extending old ones, while others are phasing them out because of concerns about accidents, waste and cost. Small modular reactors are being developed."],["Energy efficiency","Using less energy for the same result, through efficient appliances, LED lights, insulated buildings and public transport, is often called the first fuel of the transition, because the cleanest energy is the energy not used."]],
sp:["The first silicon solar cell","In 1954, three scientists at Bell Laboratories in the United States, Daryl Chapin, Calvin Fuller and Gerald Pearson, created the first practical silicon solar cell, which turned sunlight directly into electricity. At a press demonstration, it powered a toy Ferris wheel and a small radio transmitter. Newspapers predicted it could one day harness the limitless energy of the sun. At first, solar cells were so expensive that they were used mainly on satellites, starting with Vanguard 1 in 1958. Today, after decades of improvement and mass production, solar power is one of the cheapest sources of electricity on Earth."],
g:[["Energy transition","The shift from fossil fuels to low-carbon energy"],["Renewable energy","Energy from sources that are naturally replenished"],["Grid","The network that delivers electricity"],["Just transition","A fair shift that supports affected workers and communities"]],
f:["Solar panel prices have fallen about 90 percent since 2010.","The Paris Agreement was adopted in 2015.","The Philippines stopped approving new coal plants in 2020."],
fun:"The first silicon solar cell powered a toy Ferris wheel in 1954.",
tl:"Ang energy transition ay ang paglipat mula sa karbon, langis at gas tungo sa malinis na enerhiya gaya ng araw, hangin at geothermal. Layunin nitong bawasan ang mga gas na nagdudulot ng pagbabago ng klima."});

ADDART({id:"sdgs",t:"The Sustainable Development Goals",c:"Economics and Society",i:"🎯",w:"Sustainable Development Goals",r:["un","foodsecurity","inequality","climchange","education"],
p:[["Seventeen goals","The Sustainable Development Goals are 17 global goals for a better world by 2030."],["For everyone","They cover poverty, hunger, health, education, equality, climate and more."],["Mixed progress","Progress has been uneven, and many targets are off track."]],
l:"The Sustainable Development Goals, or SDGs, are 17 goals adopted by all member states of the United Nations in September 2015 as a shared plan for peace and prosperity for people and the planet by 2030. They aim to end poverty and hunger, ensure health and education for all, achieve gender equality, provide clean water and energy, create decent work, reduce inequality, build sustainable cities, protect the climate, oceans and land, and promote peace and justice. The goals have 169 targets. Progress has been mixed, slowed by the COVID-19 pandemic, conflicts and economic crises.",
s:[["Where the goals came from","The SDGs followed the Millennium Development Goals, eight goals set in 2000 for 2015, which helped reduce extreme poverty and child deaths. Unlike the earlier goals, which focused on poorer countries, the SDGs apply to all countries, rich and poor."],["The 17 goals","The goals are: no poverty; zero hunger; good health and well-being; quality education; gender equality; clean water and sanitation; affordable and clean energy; decent work and economic growth; industry, innovation and infrastructure; reduced inequalities; sustainable cities and communities; responsible consumption and production; climate action; life below water; life on land; peace, justice and strong institutions; and partnerships for the goals."],["Leave no one behind","A central promise of the SDGs is to leave no one behind, reaching the poorest and most marginalized people first, including women and girls, Indigenous peoples, people with disabilities, migrants and those in remote areas."],["Measuring progress","Countries track hundreds of indicators and report their progress to the United Nations through Voluntary National Reviews. The Philippines has presented several such reviews, linking the goals to its national development plans."]],
k:[["Adopted","25 September 2015"],["Number of goals","17"],["Number of targets","169"],["Deadline","2030"],["Predecessor","Millennium Development Goals (2000–2015)"]],
d:[["2000","The Millennium Development Goals are adopted"],["2012","The Rio+20 conference calls for new sustainable development goals"],["2015","The SDGs are adopted at the United Nations"],["2016","The SDGs take effect"],["2020","The COVID-19 pandemic sets back progress"],["2030","The deadline for the goals"]],
s2:[["Progress so far","Before 2020, the world made progress on goals such as reducing child deaths, expanding electricity access and getting more children into school. But the COVID-19 pandemic, wars and rising food and energy prices pushed millions back into poverty and hunger. UN reports in the mid-2020s found that only about a third of the targets were on track or moving moderately in the right direction."],["Criticism","Critics argue that 17 goals and 169 targets are too many to focus on, that some are vague or hard to measure, and that the goals are not legally binding. Some economists suggest concentrating resources on fewer, high-impact goals."],["Support","Supporters say the SDGs give governments, businesses, schools and citizens a common language and roadmap, encourage data collection and remind leaders of promises to the world’s poorest people. Many companies and cities now align their plans with the goals."],["The goals and young people","Schools around the world teach the SDGs, and young people campaign on issues such as climate action, education and equality. The goals’ colorful icons have become widely recognized."],["Financing the goals","Reaching the goals requires trillions of dollars of investment in poorer countries each year. Debates focus on aid, debt relief, fair taxation of multinational companies and private investment."],["After 2030","As 2030 approaches, the United Nations and countries have begun discussing what should come after the SDGs, building on what worked and what did not."]],
sp:["Everyone had a say","Unlike most international agreements, written mainly by diplomats, the Sustainable Development Goals were shaped with input from millions of people. From 2013 to 2015, the United Nations held consultations in nearly 100 countries and ran a global online survey called MY World, in which more than seven million people, many of them young people in developing countries, voted on the issues most important to them. A good education, better health care and an honest and responsive government came out on top. Their votes influenced the final list of 17 goals."],
g:[["Sustainable development","Meeting today’s needs without harming future generations"],["Target","A specific aim within a goal"],["Indicator","A measurement used to track progress"],["Marginalized","Pushed to the edges of society"]],
f:["The SDGs were adopted in 2015.","There are 17 goals and 169 targets.","The deadline for the goals is 2030."],
fun:"Millions of people voted online on which issues the global goals should include.",
tl:"Ang Sustainable Development Goals ay 17 layunin ng United Nations para sa mas mabuting mundo pagsapit ng 2030. Kabilang dito ang pagwawakas ng kahirapan at gutom, kalusugan, edukasyon, pagkakapantay-pantay at pangangalaga sa klima."});

ADDART({id:"inequality",t:"Poverty and Inequality",c:"Economics and Society",i:"⚖️",w:"Economic inequality",r:["gdp","labor","taxes","sdgs","phecon"],
p:[["Rich and poor","Inequality is the gap between those who have more and those who have less."],["Extreme poverty","Hundreds of millions of people live on just a few dollars a day."],["Progress and gaps","Extreme poverty has fallen greatly, but large gaps remain."]],
l:"Poverty is the lack of enough money or resources to meet basic needs such as food, shelter, health care and education. Inequality refers to how unevenly income, wealth and opportunities are shared within and between countries. Over the past few decades, the share of the world’s people living in extreme poverty has fallen dramatically, especially in Asia, yet hundreds of millions still live on just a few dollars a day, most of them in sub-Saharan Africa. At the same time, wealth has become highly concentrated at the top. How to reduce poverty and inequality is one of the central debates in economics and politics.",
s:[["Measuring poverty","The World Bank measures extreme poverty using an international poverty line, updated over time; in 2025 it was set at about 3 US dollars a day, adjusted for differences in prices between countries. Countries also set their own national poverty lines. Poverty can also be measured by lack of education, health care, clean water and electricity."],["A great decline","In the early 1990s, more than a third of the world’s people lived in extreme poverty. Rapid economic growth, especially in China, India and Southeast Asia, lifted more than a billion people out of it. The COVID-19 pandemic briefly reversed the trend, and progress has since slowed."],["Measuring inequality","Economists often use the Gini coefficient, developed by the Italian statistician Corrado Gini, which ranges from 0, when everyone has the same income, to 1, when one person has everything. South Africa and several Latin American countries have some of the highest levels of income inequality."],["Income and wealth","Income is what people earn; wealth is what they own, such as land, houses, savings and shares. Wealth is usually far more unequal than income. Studies estimate that the richest 1 percent of people own a large share of the world’s wealth."]],
k:[["International poverty line","About $3 a day (2025)"],["Inequality measure","Gini coefficient"],["Most extreme poverty","Sub-Saharan Africa"],["UN goals","No Poverty (SDG 1), Reduced Inequalities (SDG 10)"],["Philippine cash-transfer program","Pantawid Pamilyang Pilipino Program (4Ps)"]],
d:[["1912","Corrado Gini publishes his measure of inequality"],["1990","The World Bank introduces a global poverty line"],["2003","Brazil launches the Bolsa Família cash-transfer program"],["2008","The Philippines launches the 4Ps program"],["2020","The COVID-19 pandemic pushes millions into poverty"],["2025","The World Bank updates the international poverty line"]],
s2:[["Poverty in the Philippines","The share of Filipinos living below the national poverty line has fallen over recent decades but remains significant, especially in rural areas and parts of Mindanao. Farmers, fisherfolk and Indigenous peoples are among the poorest. Remittances, economic growth and social programs have helped reduce poverty."],["Cash transfers","Many countries give regular cash payments to poor families, often on condition that children attend school and get health checkups. Brazil’s Bolsa Família and Mexico’s programs inspired others, including the Philippines’ Pantawid Pamilyang Pilipino Program, or 4Ps, launched in 2008 and made law in 2019. Studies show such programs can improve school attendance and health."],["Causes","Poverty and inequality have many causes, including lack of education and jobs, discrimination, conflict, corruption, unequal access to land and credit, and history, such as colonialism. Economists also debate the roles of technology, globalization and tax policies in rising inequality within many countries."],["Debates","Some emphasize economic growth, free markets and entrepreneurship as the best way to reduce poverty. Others emphasize redistribution through progressive taxes, public services, minimum wages and stronger workers’ rights. Most countries combine both approaches in different ways, and views differ on the right balance."],["Inequality between countries","The average income in the richest countries is many times higher than in the poorest. Where a person is born remains one of the biggest factors in how much they earn. Gaps between countries narrowed as Asian economies grew, but many African countries have yet to catch up."],["Beyond money","Inequality also includes gaps in health, education, life expectancy, digital access and political power, and differences between men and women, cities and the countryside, and ethnic groups."]],
sp:["The elephant curve","In 2013 the economists Christoph Lakner and Branko Milanović drew a graph showing how incomes around the world grew between 1988 and 2008. It looked like an elephant. The rising back showed large gains for the middle of the global distribution, mostly people in Asian countries such as China, India and Indonesia. The dip at the base of the trunk showed little growth for the working and middle classes of rich countries. The raised tip of the trunk showed huge gains for the world’s richest 1 percent. The elephant curve became a famous picture of how globalization shaped who gained and who did not."],
g:[["Poverty","Lacking enough resources to meet basic needs"],["Inequality","Uneven sharing of income, wealth or opportunities"],["Gini coefficient","A number measuring income inequality"],["Cash transfer","Money given directly to people, often by a government"]],
f:["Extreme poverty has fallen sharply since 1990.","The Gini coefficient measures inequality.","The Philippines’ 4Ps program began in 2008."],
fun:"A famous graph of global income growth is called the elephant curve because of its shape.",
tl:"Ang kahirapan ay kakulangan sa pangunahing pangangailangan, habang ang inequality ay ang hindi pantay na hatian ng yaman. Malaki na ang ibinaba ng matinding kahirapan sa mundo, ngunit malaki pa rin ang agwat ng mayaman at mahirap."});

ADDART({id:"worldpop",t:"World Population",c:"Geography",i:"👨‍👩‍👧",w:"World population",r:["urban","megacities","agingworld","migration","indiacountry"],
p:[["Eight billion","The world’s population passed 8 billion in 2022."],["Slowing growth","Families are getting smaller in most countries."],["A peak ahead","The UN expects the population to peak later this century."]],
l:"The world’s population is the total number of people living on Earth, which passed 8 billion in November 2022. For most of history, the population grew slowly, reaching 1 billion only around 1800. Thanks to better food, medicine and sanitation, it then grew rapidly, especially in the 20th century. Today growth is slowing as families have fewer children, and the United Nations projects that the population will peak at around 10 billion in the 2080s. India became the most populous country in 2023, overtaking China, while Africa is the fastest-growing continent.",
s:[["Slow growth for millennia","For most of human history, high birth rates were balanced by high death rates from disease, famine and war. The world’s population may have been only a few million at the end of the last ice age and around 300 million in 1 CE."],["The population boom","From the 1800s, improvements in farming, medicine, vaccines and clean water allowed many more children to survive. The population reached 2 billion around 1927, 3 billion in 1960 and then added a billion roughly every 12 to 15 years."],["Smaller families","As countries become richer and more urban, and as girls’ education and family planning spread, families tend to have fewer children. The average number of children per woman worldwide has fallen from about 5 in the 1950s to around 2.2 today. In many countries it is now below 2.1, the level needed to replace each generation."],["Where people live","More than half of all people live in Asia. India and China each have over 1.4 billion people. Africa’s population is growing fastest and is expected to make up a growing share of humanity this century. More than half of the world’s people now live in cities."]],
k:[["World population","About 8.2 billion (2025)"],["Passed 8 billion","15 November 2022"],["Most populous country","India (since 2023)"],["Projected peak","About 10.3 billion in the 2080s"],["World Population Day","11 July"]],
d:[["c. 1804","The world population reaches 1 billion"],["1927","The population reaches 2 billion"],["1960","The population reaches 3 billion"],["2011","The population reaches 7 billion"],["2022","The population passes 8 billion"],["2023","India overtakes China as the most populous country"]],
s2:[["The 7 billionth baby","To mark the world reaching 7 billion people, the United Nations chose 31 October 2011 as the symbolic day. Danica May Camacho, born just before midnight in a hospital in Manila, was one of several babies around the world celebrated as symbolic 7 billionth babies."],["The Philippines","The Philippines had about 112 million people in 2024, making it one of the most populous countries in the world. Its population is young, but birth rates have fallen quickly: by 2022 the average number of children per woman had dropped below 2. A young workforce can boost economic growth, sometimes called a demographic dividend."],["Shrinking populations","Some countries’ populations are already falling, including Japan, South Korea, Italy, China and several Eastern European countries. South Korea has the lowest fertility rate in the world, well below one child per woman. Shrinking, ageing populations raise concerns about workforces, pensions and care for the elderly."],["Population policies","Some governments have tried to influence population growth. China’s one-child policy, from 1980 to 2015, limited most families to one child, and was criticized for coercion and abuses. Today many countries with low birth rates offer incentives such as child payments and parental leave to encourage larger families, with limited success."],["Population and the planet","More people need more food, water and energy, but how much people consume matters as much as how many there are. A person in a rich country often uses far more resources and causes far more emissions than a person in a poor country."],["Counting people","Countries count their populations through censuses, usually every ten years. The Philippines held censuses in 2015, 2020 and 2024. Census data help governments plan schools, hospitals and roads and divide seats in legislatures."]],
sp:["From one billion to eight","It took the whole of human history, hundreds of thousands of years, for the world’s population to reach 1 billion, around the year 1804. The second billion took about 123 years, reached in 1927. The third billion took only 33 years, in 1960. After that, a new billion was added every 12 to 15 years: 4 billion in 1974, 5 billion in 1987, 6 billion in 1999, 7 billion in 2011 and 8 billion in 2022. Because birth rates are falling, the next billion is expected to take longer, and the population may never reach 11 billion."],
g:[["Population","The number of people living in an area"],["Fertility rate","The average number of children born per woman"],["Census","An official count of a population"],["Demographic dividend","Economic growth from having many working-age people"]],
f:["The world population passed 8 billion in 2022.","India became the most populous country in 2023.","A Filipino baby was a symbolic 7 billionth baby."],
fun:"A baby born in Manila in 2011 was celebrated as one of the symbolic 7 billionth people.",
tl:"Lumampas sa 8 bilyon ang populasyon ng mundo noong 2022. Bumabagal na ang paglaki nito dahil mas kaunti na ang anak ng mga pamilya. Ang India na ang may pinakamaraming tao mula 2023."});

ADDART({id:"megacities",t:"Megacities",c:"Geography",i:"🌃",w:"Megacity",r:["urban","worldpop","manila","tokyo","lagos"],
p:[["Giant cities","A megacity is a city with more than 10 million people."],["Growing fast","Most megacities are in Asia, Africa and Latin America."],["Big challenges","Traffic, housing, floods and pollution are major problems."]],
l:"A megacity is a metropolitan area with more than 10 million people. In 1950 only New York and Tokyo were that large; today there are dozens, most of them in Asia, Africa and Latin America, including Jakarta, Dhaka, Tokyo, Delhi, Shanghai, Manila, Cairo, Lagos, São Paulo and Mexico City. Megacities are engines of economies, culture and innovation, drawing people with jobs and opportunities, but they also face enormous challenges of traffic, housing, flooding, pollution and inequality. How they are planned and managed will shape the lives of billions.",
s:[["Why cities grow","Cities grow as people move from the countryside in search of jobs, education and services, and as city families have children. More than half of the world’s people now live in urban areas, and the share keeps rising."],["The largest cities","Rankings depend on how city boundaries are drawn. In a 2025 United Nations study using a new, consistent method, Jakarta, Dhaka and Tokyo were the world’s three largest cities, each with tens of millions of people. Other giant cities include Delhi, Shanghai, Guangzhou, Cairo, Manila, Kolkata and Seoul."],["Metro Manila","Metro Manila, the National Capital Region of the Philippines, is made up of 16 cities and one municipality, with over 13 million residents and millions more in surrounding provinces. It is one of the most densely populated urban areas in the world and the country’s center of government, business and culture."],["Traffic and transport","Megacities often suffer from severe traffic jams. Metro and rail systems, bus rapid transit, safe walking and cycling paths and good planning can move many more people than private cars. Cities from Tokyo to Bogotá are known for their public transport."]],
k:[["Definition","More than 10 million people"],["Megacities in 1950","2 (New York and Tokyo)"],["Largest in 2025 UN study","Jakarta"],["Share of people in urban areas","More than half"],["Metro Manila","16 cities and 1 municipality"]],
d:[["1950","Only New York and Tokyo have more than 10 million people"],["1974","Curitiba, Brazil, opens a pioneering bus rapid transit system"],["1975","Metro Manila is created"],["2007","Half of the world’s people live in urban areas for the first time"],["2022","Indonesia passes a law to move its capital from Jakarta"],["2025","A UN study names Jakarta the world’s largest city"]],
s2:[["Informal settlements","Around a billion people live in slums or informal settlements, often without secure housing, clean water, sanitation or electricity. Many megacities include large informal communities, where residents build their own homes and economies. Upgrading these areas with services, rather than evicting residents, is increasingly seen as the best approach."],["Floods and heat","Many megacities are on coasts or river deltas, making them vulnerable to floods, storm surges and rising seas, made worse in some places by sinking land. Concrete and asphalt trap heat, making cities hotter than the countryside, an effect called the urban heat island. Parks, trees and green roofs help cool them."],["Pollution","Traffic, factories and burning waste make air pollution a serious health problem in many megacities. Delhi often has some of the worst air quality in the world in winter. Cleaner vehicles, public transport and stricter rules have improved air in cities such as Mexico City and Beijing."],["Moving a capital","Some countries have moved their capitals away from crowded megacities, such as Brazil, which built Brasília in 1960, and Nigeria, which moved its capital from Lagos to Abuja in 1991. Indonesia is building a new capital, Nusantara, on Borneo, though progress has been debated."],["Smart cities","Cities increasingly use technology, such as sensors, apps and data, to manage traffic, energy, water and services, an idea called smart cities. Critics warn about privacy and say technology cannot replace good planning and governance."],["Engines of opportunity","Despite their problems, megacities are centers of jobs, universities, hospitals, culture and new ideas. They often produce a large share of their countries’ wealth, and many people find in them opportunities unavailable elsewhere."]],
sp:["Tokyo, the city that works","Greater Tokyo has long been one of the largest urban areas on Earth, with tens of millions of people. Yet it is known for being remarkably clean, safe and efficient. Its rail network carries billions of passengers a year, with trains so punctual that delays of a minute or two can prompt apologies. At Shinjuku Station, one of the busiest in the world, millions of people pass through each day. Tokyo’s mix of dense neighborhoods, mixed land uses and excellent public transport is often studied by planners from other megacities."],
g:[["Megacity","A city with more than 10 million people"],["Metropolitan area","A city and its surrounding suburbs"],["Urban heat island","A city area hotter than its surroundings"],["Informal settlement","An area of unplanned housing, often without services"]],
f:["A megacity has more than 10 million people.","Only two cities had over 10 million people in 1950.","Metro Manila has 16 cities and one municipality."],
fun:"In 1950 only two cities in the world had more than 10 million people.",
tl:"Ang megacity ay lungsod na may mahigit 10 milyong tao. Karamihan sa mga ito ay nasa Asya, Aprika at Latin Amerika, kabilang ang Metro Manila. Malaki ang hamon ng trapiko, pabahay, baha at polusyon sa mga ito."});

ADDART({id:"agingworld",t:"Ageing Societies",c:"Economics and Society",i:"👵",w:"Population ageing",r:["aging","worldpop","japancountry","family","ofw"],
p:[["Living longer","People around the world are living longer than ever before."],["More older people","In many countries, older people make up a growing share of the population."],["New challenges","Ageing societies must plan for pensions, health care and care for the elderly."]],
l:"Population ageing is the rise in the share of older people in a population, caused by people living longer and families having fewer children. Worldwide, life expectancy has risen to over 70 years, and since 2018 there have been more people aged 65 and over than children under five. Japan, Italy, South Korea and many European countries are already super-aged societies, with more than a fifth of people aged 65 or older, and many other countries, including China, are ageing fast. Ageing reflects great successes in health, but it also raises challenges for pensions, health care, caregiving and the workforce.",
s:[["Why societies are ageing","Better nutrition, sanitation, vaccines and medicine mean that far more people survive childhood and live into old age. At the same time, falling birth rates mean fewer young people. Together, these changes raise the average age of the population."],["Super-aged societies","A society is often called aged when more than 14 percent of its people are 65 or older, and super-aged when more than 20 percent are. Japan became super-aged in the 2000s, and about three in ten Japanese people are now 65 or older. South Korea became super-aged in 2024."],["Working and retiring","As the share of older people grows, fewer workers support each retiree through taxes and pension contributions. Many countries are raising retirement ages, encouraging older people to keep working if they wish, and debating how to keep pensions affordable."],["Health and care","Older people are more likely to live with long-term conditions such as heart disease, diabetes and dementia, and many need help with daily activities. Ageing societies need more geriatric health care, home care and caregivers."]],
k:[["Global life expectancy","Over 70 years"],["Super-aged society","More than 20% aged 65+"],["Oldest country by age","Japan"],["Since 2018","More people aged 65+ than children under 5"],["UN Decade of Healthy Ageing","2021–2030"]],
d:[["1950","Global life expectancy is under 50 years"],["1982","The first World Assembly on Ageing is held"],["1991","The UN adopts Principles for Older Persons"],["2018","People aged 65+ outnumber children under five for the first time"],["2021","The UN Decade of Healthy Ageing begins"],["2024","South Korea becomes a super-aged society"]],
s2:[["Caregivers from the Philippines","Many Filipinos work abroad as caregivers and nurses for older people in Japan, Canada, Israel, Taiwan, Europe and elsewhere. Their skills and warmth are highly valued, while their own families at home often depend on the money they send. Ageing in rich countries has made caregiving a major source of jobs for migrants."],["Ageing in the Philippines","The Philippines has a young population, but the share of older Filipinos is rising. Filipino families traditionally care for elderly relatives at home. Laws such as the Expanded Senior Citizens Act give discounts and benefits, and the Centenarians Act gives a cash gift to Filipinos who reach 100, extended in 2024 to those reaching 80, 85, 90 and 95."],["Healthy ageing","Staying physically active, eating well, keeping socially connected, learning new things and getting regular health checks help people stay healthy and independent as they age. The World Health Organization promotes age-friendly cities with accessible transport, housing and public spaces."],["Technology and care","Japan and other countries are testing robots, sensors and apps to help care for older people, from robotic lifting aids to companion robots. Supporters see technology easing the shortage of caregivers; others stress that human contact cannot be replaced."],["Wisdom and contribution","Older people contribute to society as workers, volunteers, caregivers for grandchildren and keepers of knowledge and traditions. Many cultures, including Filipino culture, honor elders with gestures such as the mano, pressing an elder’s hand to one’s forehead."],["Living to 100","The number of people aged 100 or more, centenarians, is rising rapidly worldwide. Japan has the most centenarians relative to its population. Researchers study long-lived communities to understand the roles of genes, diet, activity and social connection."]],
sp:["The mano","In the Philippines, a young person greeting an older relative or elder often takes the elder’s right hand and gently presses its back to their own forehead, saying mano po. The gesture, called the mano or pagmamano, shows respect and asks for a blessing, and the elder may reply, God bless you. It is commonly done when arriving at or leaving a relative’s home and after church. The custom is a daily reminder of the respect many Filipino families give to older generations, a value that shapes how elders are cared for at home."],
g:[["Population ageing","A rise in the share of older people in a population"],["Life expectancy","The average number of years a person is expected to live"],["Pension","Regular payments to retired people"],["Centenarian","A person aged 100 or older"]],
f:["People aged 65+ outnumber children under five since 2018.","Japan is one of the oldest countries by age.","South Korea became super-aged in 2024."],
fun:"Since 2018, the world has had more people over 65 than children under five.",
tl:"Ang pagtanda ng populasyon ay ang pagdami ng matatanda dahil humahaba ang buhay at kaunti na ang anak. Ang Japan ay isa sa may pinakamaraming matatanda. Sa Pilipinas, iginagalang ang matatanda sa pamamagitan ng pagmamano."});

ADDART({id:"fastfashion",t:"Fast Fashion",c:"Economics and Society",i:"👕",w:"Fast fashion",r:["clothing","plasticwaste","labor","recycling","waterscarcity"],
p:[["Cheap and fast","Fast fashion is cheap, trendy clothing produced quickly and in huge amounts."],["Hidden costs","It has large effects on the environment and on garment workers."],["Buying better","Secondhand clothes, repairs and buying less are alternatives."]],
l:"Fast fashion is a business model in which clothing companies quickly copy the latest trends and produce large quantities of inexpensive clothes, offering new styles every week. Brands such as Zara, H&M and, more recently, online retailers such as Shein have made fashion cheaper and more accessible than ever. But the rapid production and disposal of clothes has serious costs: large amounts of water, energy and chemicals, carbon emissions, textile waste and microplastic pollution, as well as low wages and unsafe conditions for many garment workers. Consumers, governments and companies are looking for more sustainable alternatives.",
s:[["How fast fashion works","Fast fashion companies design clothes based on runway shows, celebrities and social media trends, produce them in factories mainly in Asia within weeks and sell them at low prices. Some online retailers add thousands of new items every day. Low prices encourage people to buy more and wear items fewer times."],["More clothes, worn less","Global clothing production roughly doubled between 2000 and 2014, while the number of times an item is worn before being thrown away has fallen. Many clothes are worn only a handful of times."],["Environmental impact","The fashion industry uses large amounts of water, especially for growing cotton and dyeing fabric. It produces a significant share of the world’s carbon emissions and pollutes rivers with dyes and chemicals. Synthetic fabrics such as polyester, made from oil, shed tiny plastic fibers when washed."],["Textile waste","Most discarded clothing is burned or sent to landfills, and only a tiny share is recycled into new clothes. Huge piles of unwanted clothing have accumulated in places such as the Atacama Desert in Chile and markets in Ghana, which receive secondhand clothes from rich countries."]],
k:[["Meaning","Cheap, trendy clothing produced quickly"],["Production growth","Roughly doubled, 2000–2014"],["Deadliest garment factory disaster","Rana Plaza, Bangladesh (2013)"],["Main synthetic fabric","Polyester"],["Filipino secondhand clothes","Ukay-ukay"]],
d:[["1975","Zara opens its first store in Spain"],["2000s","Fast fashion brands expand around the world"],["2008","The online retailer Shein is founded"],["2013","The Rana Plaza factory collapse kills more than 1,100 workers"],["2014","Fashion Revolution Week begins"],["2020s","Governments debate laws to limit fast fashion and textile waste"]],
s2:[["Rana Plaza","On 24 April 2013, the Rana Plaza building near Dhaka, Bangladesh, which housed several garment factories, collapsed, killing more than 1,100 workers and injuring thousands. Workers had been ordered back inside despite cracks in the walls the day before. The disaster led to an agreement between brands and unions to improve factory safety in Bangladesh and to the Fashion Revolution movement, which asks who made my clothes?"],["Garment workers","Tens of millions of people, most of them women, work in garment factories in countries such as China, Bangladesh, Vietnam, India, Cambodia and Indonesia. The industry provides vital jobs, but many workers earn low wages, work long hours and face unsafe conditions. Campaigns call for living wages and the right to organize."],["Ukay-ukay","In the Philippines, ukay-ukay shops selling secondhand clothes, many imported in bales, are popular for finding bargains and unique items. Buying secondhand extends the life of clothes, though the trade in imported used clothing also raises questions about waste and local industries."],["Slow fashion","Slow fashion encourages buying fewer, better-made clothes, choosing natural or recycled materials, repairing and mending, swapping and renting clothes, and supporting local makers. Traditional Filipino textiles, such as piña, abacá and handwoven inabel, are celebrated as sustainable alternatives."],["What companies and governments are doing","Some brands offer recycling programs, repairs and clothes made from recycled fibers, though critics warn of greenwashing, exaggerated environmental claims. The European Union and some countries are introducing rules making producers responsible for textile waste and limiting the destruction of unsold clothes."],["What you can do","Wear clothes longer, care for them well, repair them, buy secondhand, donate or swap unwanted items and think before buying. The most sustainable garment is often the one already in your closet."]],
sp:["Who made my clothes?","After the Rana Plaza disaster in 2013, the designers Carry Somers and Orsola de Castro founded Fashion Revolution, a movement to make the fashion industry more transparent and fair. Each year around the anniversary of the collapse, during Fashion Revolution Week, people around the world post photos of themselves wearing their clothes inside out, showing the labels, and asking brands on social media: who made my clothes? Garment workers, in turn, hold signs saying I made your clothes. The campaign has pushed many brands to publish lists of their factories."],
g:[["Fast fashion","Cheap, trendy clothing made and sold quickly"],["Garment worker","A person who makes clothes in a factory"],["Microplastics","Tiny pieces of plastic, including fibers from synthetic clothes"],["Greenwashing","Making misleading claims about being environmentally friendly"]],
f:["Clothing production roughly doubled from 2000 to 2014.","The Rana Plaza collapse in 2013 killed more than 1,100 people.","Ukay-ukay shops sell secondhand clothes in the Philippines."],
fun:"Each year people wear their clothes inside out to ask brands who made them.",
tl:"Ang fast fashion ay murang damit na mabilis na ginagawa at sumusunod sa uso. May malaking epekto ito sa kalikasan at sa mga manggagawa. Mga alternatibo ang ukay-ukay, pagkukumpuni ng damit at pagbili nang mas kaunti."});
